Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "shot mix",
    "offensive five changes",
    "personnel effects",
    "lineup composition",
    "shot distribution"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to quantify shot mix?",
    "How to isolate offensive five effects from opponent defense?",
    "What is the sample size of lineup stints?",
    "Can we detect small changes in shot mix?",
    "What are confounding variables?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-009",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "PULL_UP exposure-effect diagnostics. With lineup FE: slope holding shot fixed -0.0255 (0.0061); shooter's earlier scored -0.1044 vs not scored +0.0693. No lineup FE: -0.0063 (0.0036); placebo other play types -0.0075 (0.0017). Effect shrinks from -0.0255 to -0.0063 without lineup FE and same-shooter placebo is similar, consistent with shooter-level confound."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-012",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Tail/null-model audit of published suppression excess. Reproduced excess 0.4080 CI [0.378,0.438]; within-unit perm null at 0.25: obs 0.3798 null 0.3744 resid +0.0054 z 0.40; asym neg-pos 0.0176 p 0.258; drift-centered tail mass ratio 1.007. Showed that when drift, sparsity, and mechanical floor are accounted for, the excess vanishes."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF09",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Double-suppression stacking (POST_UP then PUTBACK). Baseline 1.210 n=960,764. After PUTBACK only 1.097 d=-0.113 n=15,898. After POST_UP only 1.091 d=-0.119 n=76,329. Stacked 1.138 d=-0.071 n=1,047 vs additive prediction -0.232. Inconclusive due to small sample and implementation bugs."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-021",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Penalty-state adaptation (Pilot A). any_fta 3->4 -0.151 vs placebos -5.556/-9.655; rim_pct +0.015 vs +1.666/+2.077; pts +0.020. The penalty contrast was not distinct from placebo contrasts, leaving mechanism unclear."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-009",
      "what_failed": "naive comparison without lineup fixed effects",
      "why": "confounding by shooter-level tendencies"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-012",
      "what_failed": "comparison of tail asymmetries ignoring drift, exposure sparsity, and mechanical floor",
      "why": "effect disappears when drift and null models are corrected"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF09",
      "what_failed": "test of additive stacking",
      "why": "small sample and implementation bugs"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-021",
      "what_failed": "penalty-state contrast without strong placebos",
      "why": "placebo dependence made the result indistinguishable from random variation"
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Confounding from shooter identity and lineup context can substantially bias naive estimates, requiring lineup fixed effects (FAM:SF-TMP-009).",
    "Drift, exposure sparsity, and mechanical floors can create spurious effects that vanish under proper null models (FAM:SF-TMP-012).",
    "Placebo tests are essential to rule out non‑causal variation (FAM:SF-TMP-021)."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether changing the offensive five actually moves shot mix.",
    "The size or direction of any such effect.",
    "What specific shot‑type metrics are most sensitive to personnel.",
    "The statistical power attainable with three seasons of data."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "A within‑game, within‑offense design that holds the opponent constant during a lineup change can isolate the effect of offensive personnel on shot mix.",
    "Three seasons of play‑by‑play provide enough event‑level data to achieve reasonable power, provided shot‑mix categories are aggregated into a few broad types (e.g., rim, mid‑range, three‑point).",
    "The historical failures highlight that controls must include game state, score, period, and potentially play type, and that placebo (no‑change) stints are necessary.",
    "The question as posed (\"showing the design can detect personnel effects\") leaves the exact definition of \"shot mix\" open; pre‑registration must specify the metrics and threshold for success."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Natural variability in shot mix across stints, even without a true lineup effect, is unknown and must be estimated.",
    "How many lineup‑change stints have the defense unchanged for a sufficient number of possessions is unclear.",
    "Whether the effect is large enough to be detected after multiplicity correction is uncertain."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no",
    "explanation": "No directly relevant study was found, so the fundamental decision to proceed is not based on historical evidence. However, the retrieved work informed the design by underscoring the need for lineup fixed effects, placebo tests, and drift‑aware null models."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.80,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "The number of possessions following a lineup change with the defense held constant may be small for some teams or lineup combinations, limiting power."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Opponent defensive strategy and remaining game context may still confound the pure offensive‑five effect."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Shot‑type classification from play‑by‑play may contain noise, especially for mid‑range vs. long two distinctions."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior project study directly addressed whether changing the offensive five alters shot mix. The returned families examined suppression effects and penalty‑state behaviour, not lineup‑driven shot distribution."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The test may be underpowered if the per‑lineup sample of stints is small or the effect is subtle."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Many lineup changes co‑occur with defensive changes, restricting the usable comparison window."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Game situation (score, time, period) and opponent tactical adjustments may bias the comparison."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Testing multiple shot‑type outcomes simultaneously increases the risk of false positives."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Shot‑type labels derived from play‑by‑play may misclassify some attempts, diluting the signal."
    },
    {
      "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
      "text": "Some shot‑type rates (e.g., mid‑range) may be close to zero for certain players, causing floor effects."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.25,
    "rejected": 0.15,
    "untestable": 0.10,
    "artifact": 0.00,
    "uncertain": 0.50
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The causal effect of changing the offensive five (while the defensive five and the other four offensive players remain constant) on the shot‑type distribution (rim, mid‑range, three) of the offensive team’s possessions immediately following the change.",
    "unit": "A lineup‑change stint (the sequence of offensive possessions beginning at the substitution and ending when the defensive five changes, the period ends, or a further offensive substitution occurs, capped at 10 possessions).",
    "sample": "All regular‑season and playoff games from the 2022‑23, 2023‑24, and 2024‑25 NBA seasons, excluding possessions in the final two minutes of blowouts (score differential ≥ 20) and possessions immediately after timeouts.",
    "power": "Power will be assessed by simulation using the observed within‑game variance of shot‑type proportions. The target is 80% power to detect a 2‑percentage‑point shift in three‑point rate at a Bonferroni‑corrected α = 0.05/3.",
    "controls": "Fixed effects for game × offense × defensive‑quintet, period, score differential (binned), and time remaining (binned). Additional robustness check: include play‑type (e.g., transition, half‑court) as a covariate.",
    "falsification": "1) Placebo test: select stints where the lineup change is reported but no actual substitution occurred (false events). 2) Impose a placebo change by randomly reassigning lineup‑change timestamps to other game intervals and testing for an effect. 3) Examine whether any detected shift persists when the opposing defense also changes.",
    "multiplicity": "The three shot‑type outcomes (rim frequency, mid‑range frequency, three‑point frequency) will be tested jointly using a multivariate linear model (MANOVA). Univariate follow‑ups will use Benjamini‑Hochberg correction. A global significance level of 0.05 will be maintained.",
    "decision_rule": "The design is deemed to detect a personnel effect if the global MANOVA rejects the null (p < 0.05) and at least one univariate contrast shows a significant difference in the expected direction, while the placebo tests fail to produce a significant effect. All analyses will be conducted in a blinded fashion on a split‑sample (first two seasons for exploration, third for confirmatory testing).",
    "data_version": "NBA stats API per‑game files as available on 2026‑11‑02."
  }
}

